From 301b96a84f0ea8602634890f15f3e3bedb337739 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 16:32:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] pool broker: bound the ceilings it GRANTS, not just the
 reservations it measures
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A cross-session KV-pool broker publishes an allowance document that every
agent session on the host sizes itself from. Its safety invariant summed the
reservations it could MEASURE, not the ceilings it was handing out -- so it
reported ok while granting two sessions the whole pool each.

The live stall it was found in: /v1/loads showed 421,888 of a 451,264-token
pool in use (0.9349), 1 running, 10 waiting, num_waiting_uncached_tokens
479,548 -- more than the entire pool -- cache_hit_rate 0.0, gen_throughput
0.0, and one main holding a 433,782-token prompt. The published allowance gave
EACH of two sessions 451,264 as both its window and its growth ceiling, and
still reported invariant ok=True.  [measured]

Two mechanisms, both fixed:

  * the policy returned main_grow_max = child_grow_max = pool ceiling -- the
    whole pool, per session, unconditionally;
  * the "mains are never shrunk" rail did mg = max(grow_max, window), which
    raised any bounded growth ceiling back up to the session's already-grown
    window. No policy could express "you are above your share, do not grow".

Growth was bounded per STEP and unbounded in OUTCOME, so a session ratcheted
sequentially into the whole pool.

derive_window_ceilings() now splits the LIVE max_total_num_tokens: one child
stream per granted stream is reserved first, the remainder is water-filled
across the live mains with a guaranteed primary lane, and a GRANT invariant
checks the worst case in which every session uses everything it was granted --
main_window, main_grow_max, child_window_max and child_grow_max together. At a
451,264-token pool that is 401,408 for one main, 196,608 each for two, 131,072
for three, 98,304 for four, with the grant total fitting the 406,138 usable in
every case.  [arithmetic, from the measured pool]

Reading the pool live rather than from configuration is what makes a host-KV
offload widen these numbers with no edit here -- see patch 0010, where
preferring the configured ceiling over the live pool is the bug.

A session already above its share is GRANDFATHERED rather than silently
declared fine: it keeps what it holds, is granted no growth, and the invariant
reports held_total, deadlock_risk and the pids, so a compactor shrinks it
instead of the broker ratcheting it.

The context compressor now CONSUMES main_window. Nothing read it before, so
the number was inert and the recurrence stayed reachable with no growth at
all: two mains at a 393,216-token configured window are 670,924 in-flight
tokens against 406,138 usable.  [arithmetic] A grant is adopted only while the
resident prompt still fits under the smaller trigger, so a fresh session
settles onto its share and a live full session is left alone.

BASE: this fork at commit 5a3c9f5 (see BASELINE.md).

PUBLISHED FORM. Two live process ids, in one comment and in a test's
expected `grandfathered` list, are replaced with synthetic ids (900001,
900002). Ordering is preserved, which is all the assertion depends on.

